 ## Background
-This technique was proposed as an alternative to the optical model for clipping based on the combination of volume and surface shading, which introduces a boundary layer of finite thickness around the clip object [S1, unofficial]. The boundary layer approach requires interleaved rendering, which is complex [S1, unofficial]. This method was developed to support interactive lenses and focus-and-context interfaces in volume rendering applications [S1, unofficial].
+This technique was proposed as an alternative to the optical model for clipping based on the combination of volume and surface shading, which introduces a boundary layer of finite thickness around the clip object [S1, unofficial]. The boundary layer approach requires interleaved rendering of the volume and the clip surface when using depth-based clipping [S1, unofficial]. The scheme fits into a four-pass algorithm for rendering the focus and context regions and removes most of the artifacts that result from using pre-integrated classification at the boundaries [S1, unofficial].
 
